From 359047f25c1a5889c49829529a47058fbb981749 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Albert <albert@tugraz.at>
Date: Sat, 18 Apr 2026 00:57:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fortran: Enable target preprocessor macros with -cpp
 [PR42954]

The Fortran preprocessor has never defined target-specific macros such
as __linux__, __unix__, __ELF__, __x86_64__, or __amd64__ that the C
preprocessor provides.  This was caused by the TARGET_OS_CPP_BUILTINS,
TARGET_OBJFMT_CPP_BUILTINS, and TARGET_CPU_CPP_BUILTINS hooks being
disabled since 2008 (marked as "Pandora's Box") because several target
config headers use C-family-only functions.

Fix in two parts.

Part 1: Fortran-local shims for TARGET_OS_CPP_BUILTINS and
TARGET_OBJFMT_CPP_BUILTINS.  c-family/c-cppbuiltin.cc's
builtin_define_std, builtin_define_with_value, and
builtin_define_with_int_value read the C-family-only globals parse_in
and flag_iso, so they cannot be called from the Fortran frontend.
Local helpers mirror them and take parse_in as a cpp_reader parameter
and flag_iso as a define_user bool.  A small block of #defines in
cpp_define_builtins() maps the C-family identifiers referenced by the
Linux/Darwin/ELF target config headers onto the local helpers,
allowing TARGET_OS_CPP_BUILTINS and TARGET_OBJFMT_CPP_BUILTINS to
compile in the Fortran frontend.  Unlike the C default (flag_iso=0),
the Fortran wrapper forces define_user=false so that only
reserved-namespace forms like __unix__ and __linux__ are defined, not
bare "unix" or "linux" which are valid Fortran identifiers.

Part 2: TARGET_CPU_CPP_BUILTINS equivalent for Fortran, via a shared
implementation.  The x86 CPU-macro code in gcc/config/i386/i386-c.cc
used to reference parse_in directly and relied on c-family's
builtin_define_std.  Move the pure macro-emission logic (architecture
identity, arch/tune macros, code-model macros, and the full ISA
feature block including __SSE__, __AVX__, __FMA__, etc.) into a new
file gcc/config/i386/i386-cppbuiltin.cc that takes cpp_reader as an
explicit parameter and a define_user bool instead of reading
flag_iso.  The new file is linked into cc1, cc1plus, and f951 via
c_target_objs, cxx_target_objs, and fortran_target_objs in
gcc/config.gcc, following the existing darwin-f.o and vms-f.o
precedent.  i386-c.cc keeps the #pragma GCC target handler, the
C++20 STDCPP_FLOAT16 undef (which depends on cxx_dialect), and a
thin ix86_target_macros() wrapper that delegates to the shared
implementation so TARGET_CPU_CPP_BUILTINS and i386-protos.h are
unchanged.  A new sibling hook TARGET_GENERIC_CPU_CPP_BUILTINS in
gcc/config/i386/i386.h calls the shared function from any frontend
that cannot link c-family.  gcc/fortran/cpp.cc is target-agnostic: it
only invokes the hook via an #ifdef, with no x86-specific code.
Other targets (aarch64, rs6000, ...) can opt in later by defining
their own TARGET_GENERIC_CPU_CPP_BUILTINS; no further Fortran
changes are required.

The dead __attribute__((target(...))) block in
gfortran.dg/gomp/declare-variant-10.f90 was guarded by
#if defined(__x86_64__), which was previously always false because
gfortran never defined that macro; it is now exposed and removed,
since gfortran does not support C-style __attribute__ syntax.

